<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lady of Elche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Dama de Elche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elche">Elche</a> is a limestone bust of a woman wearing elaborate jewellery and large wheel-shaped headdresses, carved by Iberian artists probably in the 4th century BCE. It was found in 1897 at La Alcudia near Elche, taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1941. It is now in the National Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche.jpg" alt="Lady of Elche" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luis Fernández García · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dama_de_Elche_-_MAN_-_20241220e.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The bust was originally painted, and its large side coils are known as rodetes. Its workmanship suggests strong Hellenistic influence, and The Encyclopedia of Religion associates it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a>, the goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> worshipped by the Punic-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>. An opening in the back indicates use as a funerary urn, and there are signs that it once belonged to a seated or standing statue, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-baza">Lady of Baza</a> or the Gran Dama Oferente of Cerro de los Santos, beside which it is shown in the museum's Iberian hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The bust came to light on 4 August 1897 on the estate of Dr. Campello, found according to the popular story by a young worker, Manuel Campello Esclapez, though the official report of the local record keeper Pere Ibarra credited Antonio Maciá. Locals called it the Moorish Queen. The French archaeologist Pierre Paris saw it and alerted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, which paid 4,000 francs, and it was sent to Paris on 30 August 1897. Moved to Montauban for safety in 1939, it came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1941 in an exchange negotiated between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> government and Franco's government, as the property of the Prado, which transferred it to the archaeological museum in 1971.</p><p>José Ramón Mélida questioned its authenticity in 1906, and in 1995 the art historian John F. Moffitt argued that it was a forgery, perhaps by Francisco Pallas y Puig. Experts in Spanish archaeology reject that view. A study of 2005 by the Spanish National Research Council found only pigments consistent with ancient materials, and in 2011 María Pilar de Luxán identified particles of human bone ash from the back cavity, supporting its use as an Iberian funerary urn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The find awakened popular interest in pre-Roman Iberian culture. The bust appeared on a Spanish one-peseta banknote in 1948, returned briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elche">Elche</a> in 1965 and again in 2006 for the opening of the city's Museum of Archaeology and History, and inspired James Tissot's paintings and Manolo Valdés's sculpture La Dama Ibérica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lady of Elche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lady of Baza and the <b>Lady of Elche</b> are thought to be the work of Iberians who knew Greek art well, and the Lady of Elche and the Lady of Guardamar show clear Hellenistic influence.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>With their jewellery and hairstyles they are thought to be the first known faces of Tartessian goddesses, and they are somewhat similar to the <b>Lady of Elche</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tartessos">Tartessos · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The stone bust of the <b>Lady of Elche</b>, carved around the 4th century BCE, comes from their territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/contestani">Contestani</a></li></ul></section>
